We need to compute P(A|B) using Bayes' theorem: P(A|B) = P(B|A) * P(A) / P(B). Given: P(A) = 20% = 0.20 (probability of bankruptcy). Of bankrupt bonds, 50% were rated good: P(B|A) = 0.50. Of bonds that went bankrupt, 70% were deemed good, meaning among all bonds that went bankrupt, 70% are rated good? Wait: "When he uses a bankruptcy prediction model, he finds that 70% of these bonds will be deemed as "good", suggesting a lower risk of failure." That sounds like among all bonds (including both bankrupt and non-bankrupt?), 70% are rated good. But the phrasing: "Of the bonds that went bankrupt, only 50% were rated as "good"." So among bankrupt bonds, 50% are rated good. The other 50% are rated bad (bankrupt). Among non-bankrupt bonds, presumably the model rates them good? The statement: "70% of these bonds will be deemed as "good", suggesting a lower risk of failure." "These bonds" likely refers to all bonds (both bankrupt and non-bankrupt). So P(B) = 0.70 (probability of a bond being rated good overall). That seems plausible: 70% rated good, 30% rated bad. So we can compute P(A|B) = P(B|A)*P(A)/P(B) = 0.50 * 0.20 / 0.70 = 0.10 / 0.70 = 0.142857... = 14.2857%. So answer: 14.3% (3 sig figs). Let's output.

The likelihood of bankruptcy given a "good" rating is  

\[
P(A|B)=\frac{P(B|A)P(A)}{P(B)}
      =\frac{0.50\times0.20}{0.70}
      =\frac{0.10}{0.70}
      \approx 0.142857.
\]

So about **14.3 %** of bonds rated "good" will go bankrupt.

**ANSWER: 14.3 %**
